# Every Go build tag used in this repo, and how CI covers it.
#
# `.github/scripts/build-tags.sh check` fails when a `//go:build` tag appears in
# the tree without an entry here, when an entry no longer matches any file, and
# when a file uses the legacy `// +build` syntax without a `//go:build` line
# (the derivation reads `//go:build` only, so such a file would be invisible).
# A new tag therefore cannot become invisible to CI by accident, which is how a
# compile break sat undetected behind `//go:build e2e` for four months (BEO-25).
#
# The guard covers accidents, not intent. The lane assertions grep ci.yml text
# and do not evaluate `if:`; the treatment column below is self-declared, so
# downgrading an entry to `default` and deleting its lane in the same commit
# passes. Reading the diff is what catches that -- this file is short on
# purpose so such a diff is obvious.
#
# Columns are tab-separated: tag <TAB> treatment <TAB> reason
#
# Rows carry no totals on purpose: a figure in prose is nothing a job derives or
# verifies, so it goes stale -- the counts this file used to carry were hand-corrected
# repeatedly. The tree is the count; `check` compares the tag and treatment columns
# against it. Do not add a total back unless a job verifies it.
#
# Treatments:
#   test-lane      a lane runs `go test -tags <tag>` over the packages carrying it
#   goos-vet       GOOS-gated; no lane on ubuntu compiles it, `GOOS=<tag> go vet` does
#   default        satisfied on ubuntu-latest, so every lane already compiles it
#   race-excluded  only ever negated; deliberately kept out of the race lane

integration	test-lane	Run by the "Integration tests" lane over the package set derived from the tag; a new tagged file joins the lane by existing.
e2e	test-lane	In internal/cli. The "Build and test" lane runs it for real rather than compile-checking, which is cheap: the suite is small and fast.
lifecycle_integration	test-lane	In internal/orchestrator. The "Build and test" lane runs it for real rather than compile-checking, which is cheap: the suite is small and fast.
windows	goos-vet	`GOOS=windows go vet ./...` compiles every windows-gated file -- test halves included, and the `windows-build-vet` gate in ci.yml now also builds and vets them natively on windows-latest -- but neither executes them, so the coverage is still compile/vet only, never a claim the windows halves were exercised (only the dispatch-only windows-observation lane runs them). It also covers internal/fleet/orphan_inventory_other.go (`!darwin && !linux`; the native gate covers it too). The `!windows` files need nothing extra: ubuntu satisfies the negation. One half is covered by no lane at all: internal/orchestrator/afk_process_identity_other.go is `!darwin && !linux && !windows`, so windows vet excludes it too. `GOOS=freebsd go vet ./internal/orchestrator/ ./internal/home/` compiles it, but the tree does not build for freebsd as a whole (internal/fleet has no such half), so that is a manual check, not a lane.
darwin	goos-vet	`GOOS=darwin go vet ./...` covers every darwin-gated file, and also covers the `darwin || linux` files through their darwin branch.
linux	default	Every `//go:build linux` file is compiled by every lane on ubuntu. The negated use inside `!darwin && !linux` is covered by the windows goos-vet entry.
race	race-excluded	Only appears as `//go:build !race` on internal/orchestrator/lifecycle_lifecycle_test.go. The lock and queue tests there measure their own timing, which the race detector distorts, so their absence from the race lane is intended rather than a gap.
